[DEBUG:] SUBSECTION: 1.1 From Traditional AI Agents to LLM-Based Agents contents before citation check: The concept of an "agent" has long been a cornerstone in the quest for artificial intelligence (AI), representing a vision of autonomous entities that can perceive their surroundings, reason about goals, and act upon the world to achieve desired outcomes. This subsection traces the evolution of this concept from its roots in classical, symbolic AI to the modern, transformative paradigm of Large Language Model (LLM)-based agents, highlighting a fundamental shift from rigid, rule-based systems to flexible, language-driven intelligences. Understanding this historical trajectory is essential for appreciating the novelty, capabilities, and inherent challenges of the current LLM-agent paradigm.

The intellectual origins of the AI agent are deeply intertwined with early cybernetics and the symbolic AI tradition of the 1950s through 1990s. Agents were conceived as computational entities designed to operate within a defined environment, possessing key properties such as autonomy, reactivity, pro-activeness, and social ability. Research in this era, as contextualized in works on the history of computational agency [Paradigms of Computational Agency], focused on developing agents with specialized knowledge and reasoning capabilities for confined domains. A seminal example is the "expert system," which encodes domain-specific knowledge as a set of logical rules. Similarly, classic robotic agents were built using a sense-plan-act cycle, where explicit perception modules built world models, planners derived a sequence of actions via logical deduction, and executors carried out those actions. Frameworks like BDI (Belief-Desire-Intention) architectures provided structured models for reasoning about goals and intentions. However, these traditional agents suffered from significant limitations. They were brittle, requiring extensive handcrafting of knowledge and rules for each specific task or domain. Their operation was largely confined to closed-world assumptions, where all necessary information was pre-defined. Scaling them to handle open-ended, dynamic real-world problems proved to be a monumental challenge, as noted in surveys on autonomous agents [A Survey on Large Language Model based Autonomous Agents; Exploring Autonomous Agents through the Lens of Large Language Models].

The limitations of purely symbolic and handcrafted approaches led to a paradigm shift towards machine learning. Agents could now learn behaviors and policies from data through reinforcement learning (RL) or supervised learning. This allowed for greater adaptability and the ability to operate in environments with inherent uncertainty. However, these learning-based agents often remained task-specific and struggled with transferring knowledge across domains. They lacked a general, human-like understanding of the world. The core problem remained: the field lacked a general-purpose, powerful foundation model that could serve as a universal starting point for building agents capable of adapting to a vast, open range of scenarios, a gap explicitly identified in foundational surveys [The Rise and Potential of Large Language Model Based Agents; Exploring Large Language Model based Intelligent Agents].

The emergence of Large Language Models (LLMs) represents the most significant recent breakthrough, providing this long-sought general foundation. LLMs, trained on internet-scale corpora of text and code, have demonstrated remarkable emergent capabilities in reasoning, knowledge recall, and language generation. Critically, as surveyed in works like "The Rise and Potential of Large Language Model Based Agents," these models offer a powerful and intuitive interface: natural language. This language-centricity fundamentally changes the agent design paradigm. Instead of relying on specialized input formats or manual state representations, an LLM-based agent can understand high-level, ambiguous human instructions and decompose them into executable steps. The LLM itself acts as a general-purpose "brain," capable of reasoning about a task, formulating plans, and generating actions, as detailed in many architectural frameworks [Cognitive Architectures for Language Agents; Exploring Large Language Model based Intelligent Agents].

This shift from rule-based or task-specific models to the LLM as a general-purpose controller is revolutionary. Traditional agents required custom architectures and knowledge bases for each new application. In contrast, a single LLM backbone can, in principle, power agents for writing code, analyzing scientific data, managing complex projects, or navigating virtual environments. This versatility is enabled by the LLM's in-context learning ability and its vast embedded knowledge, which reduces the need for explicit, handcrafted world models. However, an LLM alone is not an agent. It is a reactive text predictor. The true agentive paradigm emerges when the LLM is augmented with mechanisms for persistent memory, goal-directed planning, and the ability to interact with external tools and environments. These augmented architectures transform the LLM from a stateless predictor into a stateful, proactive entity that can maintain context over long horizons, learn from interactions, and ground its actions in the real world. This integration forms the core of the modern LLM-based agent, as systematically outlined in comprehensive surveys [The Rise and Potential of Large Language Model Based Agents; A Survey on Large Language Model based Autonomous Agents].

In summary, the evolution from traditional AI agents to LLM-based agents traces a path from specialized, brittle systems to general, adaptable intelligences. The early symbolic agents established the core tenets of agency but were constrained by their handcrafted nature. Learning-based agents introduced adaptability but lacked generality. The LLM-based paradigm, leveraging the power of foundation models and natural language, provides a scalable and flexible substrate for building agents that can operate across diverse, open-ended domains. This transition is not merely a technical improvement; it represents a fundamental reconceptualization of agent design, where language itself becomes the primary interface for defining goals, reasoning about the world, and interacting with both humans and digital systems. This history sets the stage for the detailed exploration of modern agent architectures, capabilities, and challenges that follows in this survey.
