[DEBUG:] SUBSECTION: 1.6 Scope and Structure of the Survey contents after citation check: ['The concept of an "agent" has long been a cornerstone in the quest for artificial intelligence (AI), representing a vision of autonomous entities that can perceive their surroundings, reason about goals, and act upon the world to achieve desired outcomes. This subsection traces the evolution of this concept from its roots in classical, symbolic AI to the modern, transformative paradigm of Large Language Model (LLM)-based agents, highlighting a fundamental shift from rigid, rule-based systems to flexible, language-driven intelligences. Understanding this historical trajectory is essential for appreciating the novelty, capabilities, and inherent challenges of the current LLM-agent paradigm.\n\nThe intellectual origins of the AI agent are deeply intertwined with early cybernetics and the symbolic AI tradition of the 1950s through 1990s. Agents were conceived as computational entities designed to operate within a defined environment, possessing key properties such as autonomy, reactivity, pro-activeness, and social ability. Research in this era, as contextualized in works on the history of computational agency [Paradigms of Computational Agency], focused on developing agents with specialized knowledge and reasoning capabilities for confined domains. A seminal example is the "expert system," which encodes domain-specific knowledge as a set of logical rules. Similarly, classic robotic agents were built using a sense-plan-act cycle, where explicit perception modules built world models, planners derived a sequence of actions via logical deduction, and executors carried out those actions. Frameworks like BDI (Belief-Desire-Intention) architectures provided structured models for reasoning about goals and intentions. However, these traditional agents suffered from significant limitations. They were brittle, requiring extensive handcrafting of knowledge and rules for each specific task or domain. Their operation was largely confined to closed-world assumptions, where all necessary information was pre-defined. Scaling them to handle open-ended, dynamic real-world problems proved to be a monumental challenge, as noted in surveys on autonomous agents [A Survey on Large Language Model based Autonomous Agents; Exploring Autonomous Agents through the Lens of Large Language Models].\n\nThe limitations of purely symbolic and handcrafted approaches led to a paradigm shift towards machine learning. Agents could now learn behaviors and policies from data through reinforcement learning (RL) or supervised learning. This allowed for greater adaptability and the ability to operate in environments with inherent uncertainty. However, these learning-based agents often remained task-specific and struggled with transferring knowledge across domains. They lacked a general, human-like understanding of the world. The core problem remained: the field lacked a general-purpose, powerful foundation model that could serve as a universal starting point for building agents capable of adapting to a vast, open range of scenarios, a gap explicitly identified in foundational surveys [The Rise and Potential of Large Language Model Based Agents; Exploring Large Language Model based Intelligent Agents].\n\nThe emergence of Large Language Models (LLMs) represents the most significant recent breakthrough, providing this long-sought general foundation. LLMs, trained on internet-scale corpora of text and code, have demonstrated remarkable emergent capabilities in reasoning, knowledge recall, and language generation. Critically, as surveyed in works like "The Rise and Potential of Large Language Model Based Agents," these models offer a powerful and intuitive interface: natural language. This language-centricity fundamentally changes the agent design paradigm. Instead of relying on specialized input formats or manual state representations, an LLM-based agent can understand high-level, ambiguous human instructions and decompose them into executable steps. The LLM itself acts as a general-purpose "brain," capable of reasoning about a task, formulating plans, and generating actions, as detailed in many architectural frameworks [Cognitive Architectures for Language Agents; Exploring Large Language Model based Intelligent Agents].\n\nThis shift from rule-based or task-specific models to the LLM as a general-purpose controller is revolutionary. Traditional agents required custom architectures and knowledge bases for each new application. In contrast, a single LLM backbone can, in principle, power agents for writing code, analyzing scientific data, managing complex projects, or navigating virtual environments. This versatility is enabled by the LLM\'s in-context learning ability and its vast embedded knowledge, which reduces the need for explicit, handcrafted world models. However, an LLM alone is not an agent. It is a reactive text predictor. The true agentive paradigm emerges when the LLM is augmented with mechanisms for persistent memory, goal-directed planning, and the ability to interact with external tools and environments. These augmented architectures transform the LLM from a stateless predictor into a stateful, proactive entity that can maintain context over long horizons, learn from interactions, and ground its actions in the real world. This integration forms the core of the modern LLM-based agent, as systematically outlined in comprehensive surveys [The Rise and Potential of Large Language Model Based Agents; A Survey on Large Language Model based Autonomous Agents].\n\nIn summary, the evolution from traditional AI agents to LLM-based agents traces a path from specialized, brittle systems to general, adaptable intelligences. The early symbolic agents established the core tenets of agency but were constrained by their handcrafted nature. Learning-based agents introduced adaptability but lacked generality. The LLM-based paradigm, leveraging the power of foundation models and natural language, provides a scalable and flexible substrate for building agents that can operate across diverse, open-ended domains. This transition is not merely a technical improvement; it represents a fundamental reconceptualization of agent design, where language itself becomes the primary interface for defining goals, reasoning about the world, and interacting with both humans and digital systems. This history sets the stage for the detailed exploration of modern agent architectures, capabilities, and challenges that follows in this survey.', '## 1.2 Defining LLM-Based Agents: Core Tenets and Architectural Paradigms\n\nThe integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into autonomous agent frameworks marks a significant paradigm shift from viewing LLMs as mere conversational or generative tools to positioning them as cognitive cores capable of agency. Formally, an **LLM-based agent** can be defined as an autonomous system where a large language model serves as the central reasoning and decision-making engine, enabling it to perceive its environment, formulate plans, take actions, and learn from interactions to achieve specified goals. This stands in stark contrast to the use of a **standalone LLM**, which operates in a stateless, single-turn or limited multi-turn dialogue mode, responding directly to user prompts without inherent initiative, long-term memory, or the ability to execute actions in external environments. The leap to agency is characterized by a set of foundational tenets that imbue the system with a more dynamic, persistent, and goal-oriented character.\n\nThe first and most fundamental tenet is **autonomy**. An LLM-based agent exhibits the capacity to operate with a degree of independence, making decisions and initiating actions without requiring explicit human instruction for every step. This autonomy is not absolute but is typically bounded by initial goals, safety constraints, and environmental context. As described in [The Rise and Potential of Large Language Model Based Agents: A Survey], autonomy is what allows an agent to decompose a high-level objective into a sequence of sub-tasks and manage their execution. This is enabled by the LLM\'s ability to generate intermediate reasoning steps and action plans, moving beyond the reactive, prompt-response paradigm. The architectural manifestation of autonomy often involves a core loop where the LLM generates a plan or action, an executor carries it out in the environment, and the outcome is fed back into the LLM for the next decision cycle.\n\nSecond is **goal-directedness**. Unlike a standalone LLM which primarily aims to generate a coherent and helpful response to an immediate query, an LLM-based agent is oriented towards accomplishing a specific, often long-term, objective. This goal directs the agent\'s planning, resource allocation, and interaction with tools. As noted in [Formally Specifying the High-Level Behavior of LLM-Based Agents], agents are "goal-driven," and their behaviors are structured to make progress towards satisfying these goals. The architectural implication is the necessity of mechanisms for goal specification, plan generation, and progress tracking, ensuring the agent\'s actions remain relevant and cumulatively useful.\n\n**Reactivity** constitutes the third core tenet, referring to the agent\'s ability to perceive and respond to changes in its environment in a timely manner. This environment can be digital (e.g., web pages, databases, APIs) or physical (e.g., robotic sensors). Reactivity ensures the agent can adapt its plans based on new information, such as a failed tool call or an unexpected observation. An architecture supporting reactivity integrates perception modules that translate environmental data into a format the LLM can process, often as textual descriptions or structured observations, as part of the agent\'s ongoing context.\n\nFinally, **proactivity** elevates the agent beyond merely reacting to stimuli. A proactive agent can anticipate future needs, seek out relevant information, or initiate actions to shape its environment to better achieve its goals. This tenet is closely linked to advanced planning and reflection capabilities. As explored in [A Review of Prominent Paradigms for LLM-Based Agents: Tool Use (Including RAG), Planning, and Feedback Learning], proactive behaviors can include self-initiated retrieval of knowledge (e.g., via RAG) or reflection on past actions to improve future strategies. Architecturally, proactivity is supported by modules that enable the agent to schedule internal evaluations, search for tools proactively, or simulate potential future states.\n\nContrasting this with standalone LLM use clarifies the distinction. A user interacting with a standalone LLM (e.g., via a chat interface) is the sole driver of the interaction. The LLM generates text based on the current prompt and conversation history, but it does not *do* things in the world, remember the interaction beyond the session context, or take initiative. An LLM-based agent, conversely, can be tasked with a goal like "book a business trip" and would autonomously perform web searches, compare flight and hotel options, interface with booking APIs, and handle email confirmations, all while managing its internal state and adapting to obstacles like a sold-out flight.\n\nThese tenets are woven together within a **canonical architectural paradigm** that is emerging as a standard template for building LLM-based agents. A widely adopted blueprint, as generalized in surveys like [A Survey on Large Language Model based Autonomous Agents] and [Exploring Large Language Model based Intelligent Agents: Definitions, Methods, and Prospects], typically comprises three core modules: a **Brain (LLM)**, a **Perception module**, and an **Action module**.\n\n1.  **Brain (LLM Core):** This is the central reasoning unit, responsible for understanding goals, formulating plans, making decisions, and generating language-based outputs that can be translated into actions. Its power is derived from the LLM\'s foundational capabilities in language understanding, reasoning, and knowledge.\n\n2.  **Perception Module:** This component interfaces with the environment to gather information. It may process raw data from various sensors or digital sources and convert it into a textual or structured representation that the LLM Brain can comprehend. For example, it might summarize a webpage\'s content, describe the current state of a GUI, or format API responses.\n\n3.  **Action Module:** This module translates the LLM Brain\'s decisions into concrete actions that affect the environment. These actions can range from generating natural language text (e.g., sending an email) to calling external tools and APIs, executing code, or controlling robotic actuators. The design of this module is critical for tool use, a key paradigm for extending agent capabilities beyond the LLM\'s internal knowledge, as highlighted in [A Review of Prominent Paradigms for LLM-Based Agents: Tool Use (Including RAG), Planning, and Feedback Learning].\n\nCrucially, this basic triad is augmented by essential supporting subsystems that enable the core tenets. A **Memory System** (short-term, episodic, semantic) provides the persistence required for long-term goal-directed behavior and learning. A **Planning and Reasoning Module**, often leveraging techniques like Chain-of-Thought, Tree-of-Thought, or explicit plan selection, operationalizes goal-directedness and proactivity. As detailed in [Understanding the planning of LLM agents: A survey], these modules are where strategies for task decomposition and reflective correction are implemented. Furthermore, architectural frameworks are increasingly focusing on non-functional aspects like safety and alignment, ensuring autonomy is balanced with controllability.\n\nThe emergence of multi-agent systems, as noted in [The Rise and Potential of Large Language Model Based Agents: A Survey], represents an extension of this paradigm, where multiple such autonomous architectures interact, each potentially with specialized roles, creating complex collaborative or simulated social dynamics. In summary, the definition of an LLM-based agent transcends the model itself; it is a cohesive, interactive system where the LLM provides the cognitive spark, but architectural scaffolding for memory, planning, perception, and action provides the "body" that enables true, goal-directed agency in the world.', 'The transition from traditional AI agents to LLM-based agents represents a fundamental shift, not merely in performance, but in the very nature of the agent\'s core cognitive engine. Whereas classical agents relied on specialized, often brittle, symbolic or modular components, the modern paradigm posits the Large Language Model itself as the unified, central "brain." This architectural choice leverages the unique emergent capabilities of LLMs, which serve as a general-purpose cognitive substrate, integrating reasoning, knowledge representation, and language understanding into a single, powerful module. This section elucidates the rationale behind this paradigm, focusing on why LLMs are uniquely suited to play this role, enabling a new class of autonomous, goal-directed, and interactive systems.\n\nThe primary catalyst is the emergence of sophisticated, general-purpose reasoning capabilities from scale. LLMs, when sufficiently large and trained on vast corpora, exhibit abilities that transcend simple pattern matching. They can perform multi-step deduction, analogical reasoning, and abstract thought, providing the foundational logic necessary for planning and decision-making in complex environments. Research has shown that LLMs can act as effective symbolic reasoners, successfully navigating tasks that require applying logical rules and common sense, such as those found in text-based games [Large Language Models Are Neurosymbolic Reasoners]. Furthermore, techniques like Chain-of-Thought prompting have systematically unlocked and enhanced these latent reasoning pathways, allowing models to externalize their problem-solving processes. The capacity for self-improvement is also notable; frameworks enable LLMs to autonomously discover and compose task-intrinsic reasoning structures, moving beyond fixed prompting strategies to dynamically adapt their cognitive approach [Self-Discover]. This inherent flexibility allows a single LLM-based brain to tackle a wide spectrum of reasoning challenges, from spatial problem-solving [A LLM Benchmark based on the Minecraft Builder Dialog Agent Task] to strategic interactions [LLM as A Robotic Brain: Unifying Egocentric Memory and Control].\n\nSecondly, LLMs serve as an unparalleled repository of world knowledge, accessible through natural language. Trained on a significant portion of the internet, they encode a vast and diverse commonsense and factual database. This embedded knowledge is not inert; it is dynamically retrievable and applicable to novel situations. This capability allows agents to ground their actions in a broad understanding of the world without requiring task-specific knowledge bases for every scenario. As highlighted in studies on cognitive agents, LLMs can function as external knowledge sources, providing the contextual information necessary for making informed decisions within a cognitive architecture [Exploiting Language Models as a Source of Knowledge for Cognitive Agents]. The model\'s knowledge, however, is not static. When coupled with tools like Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), the LLM\'s brain can be augmented with up-to-date or domain-specific information, creating a dynamic interplay between its parametric knowledge and external, verifiable sources [Formally Specifying the High-Level Behavior of LLM-Based Agents]. This fusion of parametric and retrieved knowledge creates a more robust and adaptable foundation for agent cognition.\n\nPerhaps most importantly, language itself is the killer interface. The LLM\'s core competency—understanding and generating natural language—provides a universal, low-friction medium for interaction with both humans and computational tools. This solves a critical bottleneck in traditional AI: the need for custom interfaces for every input/output modality. With an LLM at the helm, an agent can perceive instructions from a user in plain English, translate high-level goals into executable plans, invoke external tools via textual API descriptions, and articulate its reasoning or results back to humans seamlessly. This linguistic interface enables the integration of diverse capabilities. As research on communication formats suggests, agents can even move beyond strict natural language to autonomously develop more efficient, structured communication protocols (like code or logical expressions) when needed, further optimizing the interface layer [Beyond Natural Language: LLMs Leveraging Alternative Formats for Enhanced Reasoning and Communication]. The language interface is therefore not just a communication pipe but a flexible cognitive tool that enables the agent to read, write, reason about, and manipulate symbolic representations of the world and its own goals.\n\nHowever, viewing the LLM as a brain also reveals critical limitations that the agent architecture must compensate for. The LLM\'s reasoning, while powerful, can be superficial, biased, or prone to "hallucination." It lacks true causal understanding of the physical world and its knowledge is frozen at the time of training. Its context window, though vast, is finite, challenging true long-term memory and multi-session continuity. Consequently, the LLM-as-brain is not a standalone solution but the central component of a larger cognitive architecture. This architecture must provide the scaffolding that the brain itself lacks: **perception modules** to process multimodal sensory inputs (beyond text), **memory systems** (both short-term working memory and long-term episodic/semantic stores) to overcome context limitations and learn from experience [Empowering Working Memory for Large Language Models Agents; A Survey on the Memory Mechanism of Large Language Model based Agents], **planning and reflection mechanisms** to impose structured, goal-directed reasoning over the LLM\'s more intuitive outputs [Understanding the planning of LLM agents: A survey; Enhancing the General Agent Capabilities of Low-Parameter LLMs through Tuning and Multi-Branch Reasoning], and **grounding interfaces** to connect linguistic actions to the digital or physical world through tool use and code execution [If LLM Is the Wizard, Then Code Is the Wand: A Survey on How Code Empowers Large Language Models to Serve as Intelligent Agents].\n\nIn summary, the LLM serves as the "brain" of an agent not because it is a perfect replica of human cognition, but because it offers a uniquely versatile set of general-purpose cognitive primitives: plausible reasoning over arbitrary domains, vast associative knowledge, and a flexible linguistic interface. This combination allows for the creation of agents that are more adaptive, interactive, and capable of handling open-ended tasks than any previous specialized AI system. The subsequent sections will explore how this powerful but imperfect brain is augmented and orchestrated within larger architectures to achieve robust, autonomous, and aligned agency [A Survey on Large Language Model based Autonomous Agents].', 'The transition of large language models (LLMs) from passive text generators into active, goal-directed agents is fundamentally enabled by the integration of specialized architectural components that extend their native capabilities. While an LLM’s core proficiency lies in next-token prediction, true agentive behavior—the ability to autonomously perceive, reason, plan, act, and learn within an environment—requires a supportive ecosystem of modules. This subsection provides a brief preview of three foundational pillars that, when coupled with an LLM, endow it with agency: memory mechanisms, planning modules, and tool-use interfaces. Together, these components transform the LLM from a stateless model into a stateful, interactive, and capable entity.\n\nThe first critical component is **memory**, which provides the agent with the necessary continuity, context, and experiential knowledge to operate effectively over time and across interactions. Standalone LLMs are limited by their context window, effectively offering only short-term working memory. Agent architectures, however, implement more sophisticated memory systems inspired by human cognition. A comprehensive survey of these mechanisms distinguishes between **short-term memory**, often implemented via the LLM\'s context window for immediate conversational history, and **long-term memory**, which involves persistent storage and retrieval of past experiences, knowledge, and learned facts [A Survey on the Memory Mechanism of Large Language Model based Agents]. This long-term memory can be further categorized into **episodic memory** (specific past events and interactions) and **semantic memory** (general world knowledge and learned concepts). The architecture RAISE exemplifies a practical implementation, incorporating a dual-component memory system that mirrors human short-term and long-term memory to maintain context and continuity in multi-turn dialogues [From LLM to Conversational Agent: A Memory Enhanced Architecture with Fine-Tuning of Large Language Models]. Furthermore, advanced memory systems like Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) allow agents to dynamically retrieve relevant external knowledge, effectively grounding their reasoning and decisions in up-to-date information beyond the LLM\'s static training data [RAP: Retrieval-Augmented Planning with Contextual Memory for Multimodal LLM Agents]. Memory is not merely passive storage; it is an active component that informs reflection, enables learning from past mistakes, and provides the historical context crucial for coherent long-horizon task execution.\n\nThe second foundational pillar is **planning**, the cognitive process that enables an agent to formulate, evaluate, and execute a sequence of actions to achieve a specified goal. For LLM-based agents, planning is a core capability that moves beyond simple chain-of-thought reasoning into structured, often hierarchical, decision-making. This involves decomposing complex tasks into manageable sub-tasks, selecting optimal action sequences, and adapting plans based on feedback and new information. A dedicated survey on LLM agent planning provides a systematic taxonomy, categorizing approaches into **task decomposition**, **plan selection**, **external modules**, **reflection**, and **memory-augmented planning** [Understanding the planning of LLM agents A survey]. Planning is not a monolithic function but a suite of strategies. Some agents employ reactive planning, generating actions in direct response to stimuli, while others use deliberative planning, constructing a comprehensive plan before execution. The integration of reflection is particularly powerful, allowing agents to critique and refine their own plans or past actions, a process known as self-correction. Research also explores the synergy between LLM-based planning and traditional symbolic planning methods, suggesting a neuro-symbolic approach where LLMs generate high-level plans or heuristics that are then formalized and solved by classical planners for greater reliability and guarantee [On the Prospects of Incorporating Large Language Models (LLMs) in Automated Planning and Scheduling (APS); On the Roles of LLMs in Planning: Embedding LLMs into Planning Graphs]. This module essentially acts as the agent\'s executive function, translating high-level goals into actionable steps.\n\nThe third essential component is the **tool-use interface**, which grants agents the ability to interact with and manipulate the external world, vastly expanding their capabilities beyond text generation. Tool use allows an agent to execute code, query databases, call APIs, control software applications, and interact with physical or simulated environments. This transforms the agent from a reasoning entity into an acting entity. Frameworks like Tulip Agent demonstrate architectures where agents can dynamically discover, retrieve, and manage large libraries of tools, overcoming the limitations of encoding all tool descriptions within a fixed context window [Tulip Agent: Enabling LLM-Based Agents to Solve Tasks Using Large Tool Libraries]. A key aspect is the development of efficient tool retrieval mechanisms, such as using vector stores to search for relevant tools based on task descriptions, significantly reducing inference costs and enabling scalability [EASYTOOL: Enhancing LLM-based Agents with Concise Tool Instruction]. Tool integration itself can take multiple forms: direct API invocation, generating and executing code within a sandboxed environment (e.g., a Python interpreter), or using specialized middleware that shields the LLM from environmental complexity [Middleware for LLMs: Tools Are Instrumental for Language Agents in Complex Environments]. The tool-use interface is thus the agent\'s actuator, bridging the internal reasoning of the LLM with external effects.\n\nThese three foundational components—memory, planning, and tool use—do not operate in isolation but form a deeply interconnected cognitive loop. Memory provides the contextual and experiential data that informs the planning module. The planning module, in turn, generates the action sequences that are executed via the tool-use interface. The outcomes and observations from tool execution are then fed back into memory, closing the loop and enabling learning and adaptation. Architectures such as the one described in "LLM-Agent-UMF" provide a unified framework that explicitly delineates these components—planning, memory, and action (tool use)—alongside others like security, establishing a clear blueprint for agent design [LLM-Agent-UMF: LLM-based Agent Unified Modeling Framework for Seamless Integration of Multi Active/Passive Core-Agents]. Similarly, the modular framework proposed for multi-LLM systems decomposes tool-use capabilities into specialized planner, caller, and summarizer roles, highlighting the component-based design philosophy [Small LLMs Are Weak Tool Learners: A Multi-LLM Agent]. Understanding these foundational components is essential for analyzing any specific agent architecture, as their design, implementation, and interplay ultimately determine the agent\'s capabilities, efficiency, and reliability in performing real-world tasks.', 'The evolution of Large Language Model (LLM)-based agents represents a fundamental shift in how we conceptualize and deploy artificial intelligence for complex task-solving. This subsection traces the trajectory from the foundational single-agent paradigm to the increasingly sophisticated and collaborative multi-agent systems (MAS) that are now at the forefront of AI research and application. The core premise is that while a single LLM-powered agent demonstrates remarkable autonomous capabilities, the true potential for solving open-ended, large-scale, and socially nuanced problems is unlocked through the orchestrated collaboration of multiple specialized agents. This emergence of agency is not merely a quantitative increase in agents but a qualitative leap in system intelligence, resilience, and adaptability.\n\nThe journey begins with the **single-agent architecture**, which establishes the core components that endow an LLM with agency. This architecture typically comprises the LLM as a central "brain" augmented with modules for memory, planning, and tool use. Projects like [AgentLite: A Lightweight Library for Building and Advancing Task-Oriented LLM Agent System] have been instrumental in providing lightweight, task-oriented frameworks that simplify the development and evaluation of such agent reasoning strategies and architectures. A single agent operates within a given environment—be it a software API, a web interface, or a virtual world—perceiving information, reasoning about goals, planning sequences of actions, and executing them via tool calls or environment interactions. This paradigm has proven highly effective for bounded, well-defined tasks where a single coherent chain of reasoning can lead to a solution. However, its limitations become apparent when confronting tasks that require parallel processing of heterogeneous sub-tasks, deep domain expertise across disparate fields, robust debate and verification of information, or simulation of complex social dynamics.\n\nThe inherent constraints of the single-agent model naturally give rise to the **multi-agent paradigm**, driven by the need for enhanced capability, robustness, and scalability. As articulated in [LLM Multi-Agent Systems: Challenges and Open Problems], leveraging the diverse capabilities and roles of individual agents within a multi-agent system allows these systems to tackle complex tasks through collaboration. This transition mirrors principles from classical Multi-Agent Systems (MAS) research, where the coordination of autonomous entities is key to solving distributed problems [Distributed Constraint Optimization Problems and Applications: A Survey]. The integration of LLMs, however, revolutionizes this field by replacing traditional, rule-based agents with agents powered by general-purpose reasoning, natural language communication, and flexible planning capabilities.\n\nA primary motivation for multi-agent collaboration is **task decomposition and specialization**. Complex real-world problems are rarely monolithic; they comprise interdependent sub-tasks that benefit from focused expertise. Frameworks like [AutoAgents: A Framework for Automatic Agent Generation] explicitly address this by adaptively generating and coordinating multiple specialized agents to build an AI team tailored to a given task. Similarly, [STRATA: A Unified Framework for Task Assignments in Large Teams of Heterogeneous Agents] provides a unified framework for effectively assigning tasks within large teams of heterogeneous agents based on their traits and capabilities. This specialization allows the system to parallelize work, apply the most suitable reasoning strategy for each sub-problem, and achieve a level of performance that surpasses any single, generalized agent. For instance, in complex engineering workflows, one agent may specialize in code generation, another in verification, and a third in deployment, all collaborating under a coordinator [BMW Agents -- A Framework For Task Automation Through Multi-Agent Collaboration].\n\n**Dynamic team formation and organizational structures** represent a further evolution beyond static agent roles. The [AgentVerse: Facilitating Multi-Agent Collaboration and Exploring Emergent Behaviors] framework is inspired by human group dynamics, enabling the collaborative and dynamic adjustment of agent compositions as a greater-than-the-sum-of-its-parts system. This allows for emergent behaviors and adaptive responses to task demands. Another notable approach is the self-organizing system proposed in [S-Agents: Self-organizing Agents in Open-ended Environments], which introduces a "tree of agents" structure for dynamic workflows, enabling autonomous coordination in open and dynamic environments without human intervention. Such systems move towards mimicking the fluidity and resilience of human organizations, where teams can form, adapt, and dissolve as needed.\n\nThe effectiveness of multi-agent systems hinges critically on **communication and coordination protocols**. Unlike human teams, agents must rely on structured interaction mechanisms. This includes debate and consensus-building protocols where agents critique and refine each other\'s outputs to improve accuracy and reduce hallucinations, a challenge noted in [LLM Multi-Agent Systems: Challenges and Open Problems]. Innovative frameworks like [Autonomous Agents for Collaborative Task under Information Asymmetry] tackle the specific challenge of communication under information asymmetry, proposing mechanisms for agents to proactively exchange information necessary for task resolution. The design of these protocols determines the system\'s ability to maintain coherence, resolve conflicts, and propagate useful information while mitigating the spread of errors.\n\nAs systems scale, new **architectural patterns and emergent behaviors** come to the fore. Research into the scaling of agent collaboration networks, such as [Scaling Large-Language-Model-based Multi-Agent Collaboration], investigates whether principles akin to neural scaling laws apply to agent counts. Their work with multi-agent collaboration networks (MacNet) suggests that topologies resembling small-world properties can achieve superior performance and that a "collaborative scaling law" exists, where solution quality improves with more agents. This scaling introduces practical challenges in communication overhead, resource management, and system robustness. Investigations into system resilience, such as [On the Resilience of Multi-Agent Systems with Malicious Agents], highlight that hierarchical structures can offer superior defense against adversarial agents, underscoring the importance of architectural choices for security.\n\nFurthermore, the transition to multi-agent systems **expands the scope of application domains**. While single agents have been successfully applied in software engineering, data analysis, and creative tasks, multi-agent systems unlock potential in social simulation, large-scale automation, and complex decision-support systems. For example, [Multi-Agent Collaboration Framework for Recommender Systems] deploys a multi-agent collaboration framework directly for various recommendation tasks, moving beyond using agents merely for user/item simulation. Similarly, [A Multi-Agent System of Project Bidding Management Simulation] uses MAS to investigate complex management strategies in construction. The ability to simulate human social and organizational dynamics is a particularly potent application, as explored in works that simulate negotiation teams [Tasks for agent-based negotiation teams: Analysis, review, and challenges] and collaborative construction tasks [Multi-Agent Collaboration for Building Construction].\n\nIn conclusion, the emergence of agency in LLM-based systems marks a progressive journey from autonomous single entities to interconnected, collaborative societies of agents. This evolution is driven by the need to transcend the limitations of individual models in handling complexity, uncertainty, and scale. The core architectural advances—from specialization and dynamic teaming to sophisticated communication protocols and scalable network designs—are transforming LLMs from powerful reasoning engines into the building blocks of next-generation cooperative intelligent systems. This paradigm shift not only amplifies problem-solving capabilities but also opens new frontiers in simulating and understanding the very fabric of collaboration itself.', '本小节将明确界定本综述的范围，并系统阐述后续各章节的内容与组织结构，旨在为读者提供一份关于大语言模型智能体（LLM-based Agents）这一快速演进领域的全景式导航。本综述的核心聚焦于由大型语言模型作为核心推理与决策引擎（即“大脑”）所驱动的智能体系统。我们将系统性地审视此类智能体的定义、核心架构、关键认知能力、与外部世界的交互方式、多智能体协作模式、广泛的应用领域、评估方法论、所面临的严峻安全与伦理挑战，以及未来的发展蓝图。与聚焦于特定应用（如自动驾驶）或单一技术（如提示工程）的综述不同，本文旨在提供一个全面、连贯且具有前瞻性的视角，覆盖从基础理论到实践挑战的完整链条。本综述的方法论遵循软件工程领域对系统性文献综述的严谨要求[An Empirically Evaluated Checklist for Surveys in Software Engineering]，力求在广度和深度上取得平衡，为研究者和实践者提供有价值的参考框架。\n\n为了实现这一目标，本综述的结构遵循逻辑递进的原则，从基础概念到复杂系统，从能力构建到社会影响。**第二部分“核心架构与设计范式”** 将深入剖析构建智能体的技术蓝图。该部分首先将系统性地回顾构成智能体的基石：记忆系统（包括短期、情景与语义记忆）与感知系统如何处理输入，以及规划、推理与反思模块如何实现战略性思考与元认知。随后，我们将审视单智能体设计中的经典范式（如反应式、审思式与混合式），并进一步探讨多智能体系统的组织架构，包括角色分配、通信协议与协调机制。至关重要的是，该部分将提炼出一系列确保智能体负责任且对齐的设计原则，这借鉴了在复杂安全关键系统（如自动驾驶、航空）中为平衡自主性与安全性而发展出的架构思想[Architecting Safer Autonomous Aviation Systems]。\n\n在奠定架构基础之后，**第三部分“核心认知能力：推理、规划与反思”** 将聚焦于赋予智能体“思考”能力的技术内核。本部分将追溯推理技术从思维链到更复杂结构的演进，并深入调研多样化的规划策略与算法，以及旨在从错误中学习、实现自我修正的反思机制。最终，我们将探讨如何将这些能力整合为更整体的认知架构，例如受双过程理论启发的模型，从而为智能体提供更接近人类的决策框架。\n\n智能体的力量不仅在于思考，更在于行动。因此，**第四部分“工具增强、具身化与环境交互”** 将详细阐述智能体如何通过与外部世界的交互来扩展其能力边界。这包括动态工具发现与检索的框架、通过API、代码生成与解释器实现工具集成的机制，以及利用检索增强生成技术将动作和计划根植于外部知识库。此外，本部分将广泛讨论智能体与数字环境（如软件和网络）、具身物理环境（如机器人）乃至混合现实系统的交互挑战与解决方案，这些挑战和解决方案在自动驾驶等复杂系统的安全与性能评估中已被深入研究[Evaluation Framework for Performance Limitation of Autonomous Systems under Sensor Attack]。\n\n当智能体的数量增多时，系统复杂性呈指数增长。**第五部分“多智能体协作与社会模拟”** 将转向研究多个智能体如何通过协作与沟通来解决单个智能体无法完成的复杂任务。该部分将分析协作框架、通信策略，并特别关注智能体社会中涌现出的信任、合作等社会行为。这些研究不仅对构建高效协作系统至关重要，也为在受控环境中模拟和研究人类社会动态提供了全新范式。\n\n理论与架构的最终价值体现在其解决实际问题的能力上。**第六部分“跨专业领域的应用”** 将提供一个精选的应用全景图，展示智能体在软件工程、科学研究、医疗健康、数据分析、机器人技术以及创意开放域等多个关键领域的实际应用与影响力。这些应用案例将具体说明不同架构如何被适配以应对特定领域的独特挑战，例如安全关键性、实时性要求或开放性目标。\n\n任何新兴技术的成熟都离不开严谨的评估。**第七部分“评估、基准测试与方法论”** 将批判性地审视当前评估智能体的范式，讨论超越传统准确性的新维度（如鲁棒性、效率与安全性），并分析评估交互式、长时程任务所面临的独特挑战。该部分将特别关注向环境与仿真基准的转变，以及为实现可扩展、自动化评估所提出的新框架[SCOPE: C3SR Systems Characterization and Benchmarking Framework]。\n\n随着智能体获得更高的自主性，其潜在风险也同步放大。**第八部分“安全、对齐与伦理挑战”** 将直面这一核心议题。本部分将系统性地分类智能体特有的安全漏洞（如提示注入攻击）、审视其在感知与交互过程中引发的隐私风险，并深入探讨将伦理原则嵌入智能体行为的困难。我们将剖析在具有长期规划能力的自主系统中实现价值对齐所面临的挑战，并回顾现有的安全评估框架与防御机制，这些在安全关键系统中已被广泛研究[Proceedings of the 3rd International Workshop on Autonomous Systems Safety]。\n\n最后，**第九部分“未来方向与开放问题”** 将基于对当前局限性的综合分析，展望该领域的前沿。这包括对可泛化与具身智能体的追求、构建可扩展的动态多智能体生态系统、设计持续高效的学习范式，以及开发更值得信赖、安全且对齐的架构。我们将探讨如何深化智能体与人类及社会系统的集成，以应对未来可能出现的、更复杂的跨域协同与社会技术挑战。\n\n通过以上八个核心部分的深入探讨，本综述旨在构建一个关于LLM-based Agents的完整知识图谱，不仅梳理已有的成就，更致力于揭示未知的疆域，为未来的创新研究与负责任的发展指明方向。']





